}


An ordinal family is the exception that IS enforced. Its \code{link}
names the cumulative distribution function the thresholds are read
through, not a link on a mean, so only a link whose inverse maps
onto the unit interval can serve. The rest are refused by name.

\code{acat()} is refused for a different reason, and it is the one place
in this table where frmtmb takes less than brms. Probit, cloglog,
cauchit and softit all map onto the unit interval and brms accepts
every one of them for \code{acat}, so the refusal is not about the link.
It is about the density. \code{brms:::inv_link_acat()} branches: on the
logit a category probability is \code{c(1, cumprod(exp(x)))} normalized,
which is the log-linear form frmtmb implements, and off the logit it
is a product of distribution functions times a reversed product of
survivals, normalized. The second form agrees with the first when
the distribution function is logistic, so it generalizes the same
model rather than replacing it, but it is a second expression that
has to be written and taped. Substituting a distribution function
into the log-linear form does not reach it, which is why the other
three ordinal families could be routed through this registry and
\code{acat()} could not. \code{acat()} says all of this when it refuses.
}

\section{Links for the other distributional parameters}{


\verb{link_<dpar>} takes the values brms allows for that parameter, and
the set is fixed by what the parameter must stay inside. The first
link listed is the default.\tabular{lll}{
   Parameter \tab Argument \tab Links \cr
   \code{sigma}, \code{shape}, \code{phi}, \code{kappa}, \code{beta}, \code{ndt}, and the \code{nu} of \code{compois} \tab \code{link_sigma} and so on \tab \code{log}, \code{identity}, \code{softplus}, \code{squareplus} \cr
   \code{nu} of \code{student} \tab \code{link_nu} \tab \code{logm1}, \code{identity} \cr
   \code{zi}, \code{hu}, \code{quantile} \tab \code{link_zi}, \code{link_hu}, \code{link_quantile} \tab \code{logit}, \code{identity} \cr
   \code{alpha} of \code{skew_normal} \tab \code{link_alpha} \tab \code{identity}, \code{log}, \code{softplus}, \code{squareplus} \cr
}


\code{identity} is in every one of those sets because brms puts it there.
It lets a parameter that must stay positive go negative, which the
density then reports as a \code{NaN} rather than as a bad link. It is
offered so that a brms model ports, not because it is a good choice.

The \code{power} of \code{tweedie()} takes no argument. It is confined to the
open interval from one to two, \code{power12} is the only link in the
roster that maps onto it, and brms has no tweedie to port from.

A prior class is a distributional parameter name, so changing a
parameter's link changes where its prior sits. \code{set_prior(class = "sigma")} is a density on sigma itself, and the placement \code{frm()}
gives it is read from sigma's link, so changing \code{link_sigma} changes
the Jacobian that prior carries. See \code{\link[=set_prior]{set_prior()}}.
}

\section{What a robust field buys}{


An inverse link SATURATES. \code{plogis(40)} is exactly one in double
precision, and so is \code{1 - exp(-exp(4))}. A density written over
\code{1 - mu} then reads \code{log(0)}, which is \code{-Inf} with an unusable
gradient, where the true log density is an ordinary \code{-40}. The
linear predictor never saturated, so a link that carries a robust
field recovers the quantity the density actually needs from the
linear predictor directly: \code{logit_eta} gives the log odds and
\code{log_eta} the log mean.

The field is present only where the plain round trip measurably
saturates AND an exact form exists. \code{cauchit} has neither the
problem nor a cure: its tails are polynomial, so the plain round
trip keeps eight digits out to a linear predictor of 3.2e9.
\code{inverse} and \code{1/mu^2} cannot overflow or underflow at any
representable positive linear predictor.

Nothing has to be done to use this. A family whose link carries the
field is put on the robust form of its density automatically:
\code{binomial()} on \code{dbinom_robust()}, \code{negbinomial()} on
\code{dnbinom_robust()}.
}

\section{Custom links}{


Wherever a link is taken, a list is taken instead of a name. It must
carry \code{name}, \code{linkfun}, \code{linkinv} and \code{mu_eta}, the derivative of
\code{linkinv}, which \code{predict(se.fit = TRUE)} and every delta-method
interval read. \code{logit_eta} and \code{log_eta} are optional; supply one
only if it is exact.
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)
d <- data.frame(x = rnorm(80))
d$y <- rbinom(80, 1, pnorm(0.4 + 0.8 * d$x))

# the mean on a probit rather than a logit
fixef(frm(bf(y ~ x), family = bernoulli(link = "probit"), data = d))$mu

# a link on a parameter that is not the mean
d$z <- rnorm(80, 1 + d$x, exp(0.2 + 0.3 * d$x))
frm(bf(z ~ x, sigma ~ x), family = student(link_sigma = "log"), data = d)
}
